# Base-Screen Focus Warning

Status: proposed

## Purpose

Warn when a live example leaves Dwarf Fortress on a different base-game
viewscreen or base-game focus than it had before the example began.

The warning is test-hygiene evidence, not a test failure. DwarfSpec-owned
components must be removed before the final observation, and their temporary
DFHack screens must not count as base-game focus changes.

## Behavioral contract

- Capture the initial base-game screen after DwarfSpec's pre-example reset and
  before any project `before_each` hook or example body runs.
- Run project setup, the example body, and project teardown normally.
- Drain example-scoped cleanup, including implicit cleanup for every current
  `ds.mount()` or `ds.mountNativeScreen()` attachment.
- Wait for the existing one-frame cleanup settlement before observing final
  focus.
- Resolve the base-game screen with
  `dfhack.gui.getDFViewscreen(true)`. Do not use the temporary topmost DFHack
  screen as the comparison identity.
- Capture a detached diagnostic snapshot containing the exact base-screen
  identity, a stable base-screen type label, and a copied focus-string list
  from `dfhack.gui.getFocusStrings(base_screen)`.
- Compare base-screen identity exactly and compare focus strings as a
  canonicalized set so that incidental list ordering does not produce a
  warning.
- Emit exactly one warning when the base-screen identity or canonical focus
  set changed.
- Include the example name, before/after screen types, before/after focus
  strings, and whether screen identity, focus, or both changed.
- If either observation is unavailable, emit one nonfatal verification
  warning instead of silently treating the state as unchanged.
- Do not restore or navigate the base-game UI automatically.
- Do not change Busted counts, terminal run state, process exit status,
  cleanup confirmation, or mount-cleanup verification because of a warning.

## Non-goals

- Do not treat ordinary DwarfSpec-owned `gui.ZScreen` hosts as base-game
  screens.
- Do not warn merely because an explicitly mounted component existed during
  the example.
- Do not add an allowlist, suppression API, or configuration option until
  observed usage demonstrates a need.
- Do not broaden this work into restoration of arbitrary base-game UI state,
  pointer state, map position, selection, pause state, or simulation state.
- Do not change the thin `tools/Run-AutomationTests.ps1` command wrapper.

## Phase 1: Isolate base-screen observation and comparison

- [ ] Add
  `src/dwarfspec/automation/base_screen_focus_guard.lua` with injected DFHack
  GUI dependencies so the behavior is unit-testable without a live game.
- [ ] Represent each observation as detached data plus a private live screen
  identity used only for in-process comparison.
- [ ] Produce stable screen labels without serializing userdata addresses.
- [ ] Copy and canonicalize focus strings without mutating DFHack-owned
  tables.
- [ ] Distinguish `screen_identity_changed`, `focus_changed`, and
  `verification_unavailable` results.
- [ ] Format bounded warning details suitable for both terminal output and the
  structured event journal.
- [ ] Add LuaDoc comments for every new or changed function, class, and
  class-like record.

### Phase 1 tests

- [ ] Add `tests/unit/base_screen_focus_guard_spec.lua`.
- [ ] Prove identical identity and focus produce no change.
- [ ] Prove a different screen instance with the same type and focus is a
  screen change.
- [ ] Prove a changed focus set on the same screen is a focus change.
- [ ] Prove focus ordering alone is ignored.
- [ ] Prove returned observations do not retain or expose mutable focus
  tables.
- [ ] Prove missing screens, failed GUI calls, and malformed focus values
  produce bounded verification-unavailable results.

### Phase 1 gate

- [ ] Focused guard unit tests pass.
- [ ] Full unit suite passes.
- [ ] Lua syntax and formatting checks pass.

## Phase 2: Place the guard around the complete example lifecycle

- [ ] Split the current combined lifecycle-hook registration in
  `src/dwarfspec/automation/host.lua` so ordering is explicit.
- [ ] Register the internal pre-example hook before loading project specs.
- [ ] In the pre-example hook, run `reset('before example')` first and then
  capture the initial base-screen focus.
- [ ] Register the internal post-example hook only after project specs have
  been discovered and loaded.
- [ ] Ensure nested and top-level project `after_each` hooks run before the
  internal post-example hook.
- [ ] In the post-example hook, run `reset('after example')`, including its
  cleanup settlement wait, before capturing and comparing final focus.
- [ ] Retain per-example state only until the post-example comparison
  completes, including when the example or a project hook fails.
- [ ] Ensure a failed DwarfSpec reset remains a cleanup error and is not
  replaced or hidden by focus-warning logic.
- [ ] Ensure emergency run cleanup and abort paths do not emit a misleading
  per-example mismatch after the example lifecycle can no longer complete.

### Phase 2 tests

- [ ] Extend `tests/unit/automation_host_spec.lua` to prove pre-hook ordering:
  DwarfSpec reset, initial capture, project setup, and example.
- [ ] Prove post-hook ordering: example, project teardown, DwarfSpec reset and
  mount cleanup, settlement wait, then final capture.
- [ ] Include top-level and nested project hooks in the ordering proof.
- [ ] Prove the comparison still runs after ordinary example failure and
  project teardown failure when Busted continues lifecycle hooks.
- [ ] Prove mount cleanup occurs before final capture.
- [ ] Prove reset failure preserves the cleanup error as the authoritative
  failure.

### Phase 2 gate

- [ ] Focused automation-host unit tests pass.
- [ ] Existing mount lifecycle and combined example/cleanup failure fixtures
  remain green.
- [ ] Full unit suite passes.
- [ ] Lua syntax and formatting checks pass.

## Phase 3: Add structured nonfatal warning reporting

- [ ] Add a generic immutable `warning.recorded` event identifier in
  `src/dwarfspec/automation/event_types.lua`.
- [ ] Define and validate a bounded warning payload in
  `src/dwarfspec/automation/events.lua` with the example name, warning kind,
  message, and detached detail content.
- [ ] Append one stable `WARNING ...` line to retained run logs.
- [ ] Publish the matching warning event exactly once through the active
  generation-owned event publisher.
- [ ] Format warning events in `src/dwarfspec/report.lua` so live CLI output
  and retained logs clearly identify the affected example.
- [ ] Keep warnings out of `failure_details` and all success, failure, error,
  and pending counters.
- [ ] Preserve `dwarfspec.event.v1`, `dwarfspec.run.v2`, and
  `dwarfspec.result.v2` validation unless the existing schemas require an
  explicit compatible extension.

### Phase 3 tests

- [ ] Extend `tests/unit/automation_events_spec.lua` for valid and invalid
  warning payloads.
- [ ] Extend `tests/unit/automation_host_spec.lua` for exactly-once warning
  publication and retained log output.
- [ ] Extend `tests/unit/process_report_spec.lua` for terminal warning
  formatting and event ordering before the affected test finishes.
- [ ] Extend result, schema, runner, and service fixtures wherever their
  exhaustive event inventories require the new event type.
- [ ] Prove a warning leaves test totals, terminal state, cleanup confirmation,
  and process success unchanged.
- [ ] Prove observation failure emits a verification warning without masking
  an existing example or cleanup failure.

### Phase 3 gate

- [ ] Focused event, host, report, runner, and schema unit tests pass.
- [ ] Full unit suite passes.
- [ ] Lua syntax and formatting checks pass.

## Phase 4: Prove behavior in live DFHack

- [ ] Add a focused live spec named
  `tests/automation/base_screen_focus_warning_live_spec.lua`.
- [ ] Capture the test-owned suite baseline and arrange suite-level
  restoration so warning scenarios cannot pollute DFHack after the focused
  run.
- [ ] Prove an example that preserves its base screen and focus emits no
  warning.
- [ ] Prove an ordinary mounted widget is automatically unmounted before the
  final observation and emits no warning.
- [ ] Prove a mounted overlay widget or complete `gui.ZScreen` does not count
  as a base-game focus change.
- [ ] Prove `ds.mountNativeScreen()` attachment cleanup does not dismiss or
  alter the borrowed base screen and emits no warning by itself.
- [ ] Navigate through normal public DwarfSpec input to a different base-game
  window or focus and deliberately leave it there until post-example
  observation.
- [ ] Prove that scenario passes, emits exactly one warning with correct
  before/after details, and still reports `cleanup_confirmed=true`.
- [ ] Prove restoration performed in a project `after_each` hook suppresses
  the warning.
- [ ] Prove the original suite-level base screen and focus are restored after
  the live fixture completes.

### Phase 4 gate

- [ ] Focused live spec passes with expected warnings and
  `cleanup_confirmed=true`.
- [ ] Full live automation suite passes with no unexpected warnings.
- [ ] Full unit suite passes.
- [ ] Lua syntax and formatting checks pass.
- [ ] `git diff --check` passes.

## Phase 5: Document and close the feature

- [ ] Document automatic base-screen focus warnings in
  `docs/writing-tests.md`, including teardown restoration guidance and mounted
  component exclusions.
- [ ] Document warning output and nonfatal exit semantics in
  `docs/command-line.md`.
- [ ] Add the feature under `Unreleased` in `CHANGELOG.md`.
- [ ] Mark the linked Testing Features item in `docs/project.todo` complete
  only after every implementation and verification gate above is satisfied.
- [ ] Record focused unit, full unit, focused live, full live, syntax,
  formatting, process-result, and cleanup evidence below.

